Straightness measurements
-------------------------
The metric is maximum XY distance from the trajectory samples to the chord
joining that trajectory's first and last samples.

Initial trajectory:
  median maximum deviation: 0.227 m
  maximum deviation:        1.014 m

Final optimized trajectory:
  median maximum deviation: 0.204 m
  maximum deviation:        0.577 m

Finding: curvature already exists before collision optimization. On median,
the optimizer reduces rather than creates the deviation (0.227 -> 0.204 m).

Filtered/segmented global path:
  while more than roughly 2 m remained, whole-message chord deviations were
  commonly 0.43-0.78 m; only the final 9-point path fell to 0.088 m.
Finding: the reference supplied to SCAN is not geometrically straight over
most of this interval, even though the physical area is flat.

Internal collision evidence
---------------------------
The planner emitted 33 A-star line strips. Logs include OPEN_SET_EMPTY,
local-target adjustment, first-three-control-points occupied, and robot-inside-
obstacle events. Thus internal collision handling is active even when the RViz
view appears clear. Published inflated clouds contained a median of 35,664
voxels per frame. For several published trajectories, a sampled center/front/
rear body point was within 0.014-0.05 m of a published inflated voxel.

However, internal collision handling is not the primary source of the normal
flat-ground curvature because the initial path is already curved before the
optimizer runs.

Odometry twist-frame test
-------------------------
body_pose header.frame_id: camera_init
body_pose child_frame_id:  base_link
The adapter changes the child frame and composes the LiDAR-to-base pose, but it
copies the input twist without rotating it or applying the lever-arm velocity.
The FSM then consumes twist.linear directly as a world-frame start velocity.

Comparison against finite-difference world position velocity:
  raw twist direction error median:          20.98 deg
  raw twist direction error 90th percentile: 157.64 deg
  yaw-rotated twist error median:             10.01 deg
  yaw-rotated twist error 90th percentile:    33.11 deg
  finite-difference speed median:              0.584 m/s
  reported twist speed median:                 0.696 m/s

Primary cause
-------------
The min-snap initializer is constrained by a start velocity expressed in the
wrong coordinate frame. It must bend the initial trajectory to satisfy that
velocity before converging toward the local target. The global reference path
is also curved, and deterministic failures may invoke a random midpoint retry,
adding further variation. Collision rebound is a secondary contributor in
frames where the internal inflated map reports occupancy.

Recommended corrections
-----------------------
1. Define and enforce twist semantics in real_go2_input_adapter. If LIO twist
   is base/sensor-frame velocity, rotate it into camera_init before publishing
   body_pose, and include omega cross lidar_to_base translation if appropriate.
2. Add a diagnostic comparing reported twist against pose finite differences;
   reject or zero start velocity when direction/magnitude is inconsistent.
3. In reference-path mode, project the start velocity onto the local reference
   tangent or use a bounded tangent speed instead of an unrestricted XY vector.
4. Log deterministic versus random initialization. Do not use random retry for
   non-collision failures on clear flat ground.
5. Preserve the exact local polyline/tangent instead of fitting the remaining
   path with a global min-snap curve when strict straightness is required.
6. Publish the exact front/rear collision-query voxels so RViz shows the same
   occupancy decision used by the optimizer.
